                        "severity": "Medium",
                        "reasoning": "The class implements 'Serializable' without defining a 'serialVersionUID'. In Java, if a class is serialized and deserialized, the JVM uses the 'serialVersionUID' to ensure compatibility. If not explicitly defined, the JVM calculates one based on class members. If the class structure changes, deserialization will fail, potentially leading to a Denial of Service (DoS). Furthermore, implementing 'Serializable' on a data-carrying class makes it a potential gadget for insecure deserialization attacks if the application uses 'ObjectInputStream' to deserialize untrusted data containing this class.",
                        "mitigation": "If serialization is not strictly required, remove 'implements Serializable'. If it is required, explicitly define a 'private static final long serialVersionUID' and ensure that any deserialization process uses a look-ahead ObjectInputStream (e.g., SerialKiller or similar) to validate the class type before instantiation.",
